  <p><strong>How can buyers test a zipped activewear pocket for phone fit, bounce and comfort?</strong></p>

  <h2>Specify the object and the opening</h2>
  <p>Tell the supplier the dimensions and weight of the object the pocket must hold, including a phone case if applicable. Ask for a clear opening measurement between the points where a phone can actually pass. A roomy pocket bag is of little use if the opening is too short or tight once the garment is stretched over a body.</p>

  <h2>Test access and stability while wearing the garment</h2>
  <p>Try opening and closing the pocket one-handed with the phone inside. Jog, sit and squat. Check whether the bag swings, pulls down the waistband or moves into the hip crease. Compare placement on representative sizes, since the same position can feel different when the waistband or leg panel is under tension.</p>

  <h2>Inspect the zipper as part of the assembly</h2>
  <p>Press against the slider and ends where they sit near skin, and run the slider repeatedly to check for lining snags. After the intended wash and dry process, inspect the sewn ends, tape and pocket-bag attachment. Record the approved trim reference, puller orientation and measurement points before bulk. A covered zipper alone does not substantiate a waterproof pocket claim.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.smbsports.com/product/mens-2-in-1-running-shorts-with-compression-liner-and-zipper-pocket">SMBSports’ example of separate zipped storage and liner storage</a>.</p>
